Delicious dear So tell us, how is soccer?

7
00:02:23,380 --> 00:02:25,380
Um, I did okay, Mom.

8
00:02:25,980 --> 00:02:26,837
Some more rice?

9
00:02:26,920 --> 00:02:30,685
It's really important that you
eat well for your brain and body.

10
00:02:32,820 --> 00:02:36,173
You may not see it, but you're
growing all the time, son.

11
00:02:37,580 --> 00:02:40,100
I'll have another half a bowl, please.

12
00:02:40,900 --> 00:02:41,730
Sure.

13
00:02:43,220 --> 00:02:46,277
Oh, Ken, tomorrow's soccer
practice has been postponed.

14
00:02:46,360 --> 00:02:48,007
This beef stew is delicious.

15
00:02:48,100 --> 00:02:48,930
Can I have some more?

16
00:02:49,013 --> 00:02:50,157
It's chicken parmesan.

17
00:02:50,240 --> 00:02:51,122
Oh, never mind.

18
00:02:53,920 --> 00:02:54,750
Phew.

19
00:02:55,160 --> 00:02:56,219
What's the matter?

20
00:02:56,560 --> 00:02:57,680
What is it, Ken?

21
00:02:58,480 --> 00:02:59,437
Oh, nothing really.

22
00:02:59,520 --> 00:03:01,285
I just ate too much at dinner.

23
00:03:01,780 --> 00:03:03,427
That doesn't sound like you.

24
00:03:04,600 --> 00:03:07,637
Normally, I just eat one bowl,
but my folks seem concerned.

25
00:03:07,720 --> 00:03:10,220
So, I eat a little more than I usually do.

26
00:03:10,660 --> 00:03:12,425
You ate more for your parents?

27
00:03:12,960 --> 00:03:14,901
Who would have ever thought that?

28
00:03:15,040 --> 00:03:16,197
Boy, were they happy.

29
00:03:16,280 --> 00:03:18,717
I might have to start
eating like this every day.

30
00:03:18,800 --> 00:03:19,630
Uh-oh.

31
00:03:19,713 --> 00:03:22,129
In that case, there's
only one thing to do.

32
00:03:22,700 --> 00:03:23,740
Jumping jacks!

33
00:03:24,020 --> 00:03:26,380
Come on, Ken! Feel the burn!

34
00:03:26,560 --> 00:03:27,560
Crunch those abs!

35
00:03:30,540 --> 00:03:31,757
All right, all right.

36
00:03:31,840 --> 00:03:32,899
You're bumping up.

37
00:03:33,120 --> 00:03:34,355
Let's get some sleep.

38
00:03:47,660 --> 00:03:48,820
What is it, Ken?

39
00:03:49,500 --> 00:03:50,580
Hey, who are you?

40
00:03:51,540 --> 00:03:53,952
Emperor, are you having trouble sleeping?

41
00:03:54,200 --> 00:03:55,965
That's because you're through.

42
00:03:56,920 --> 00:03:57,750
What?

43
00:03:57,833 --> 00:03:58,663
Who are you?

44
00:03:58,746 --> 00:04:00,151
You were just so useless.

45
00:04:00,640 --> 00:04:02,360
What are you talking about?

46
00:04:02,620 --> 00:04:04,937
And what do you think you know about me?

47
00:04:05,020 --> 00:04:06,960
You had so much potential.

48
00:04:10,300 --> 00:04:15,040
Ken, you had a chance for true
greatness to rule in infamy forever.

49
00:04:15,220 --> 00:04:17,100
And you wasted it.

50
00:04:17,420 --> 00:04:18,800
How unfortunate.

51
00:04:25,800 --> 00:04:27,080
Is she good?

52
00:04:27,880 --> 00:04:29,520
I hope so.

53
00:04:32,900 --> 00:04:37,217
Yo, Lee, I'm so tired of lifting this
paintbrush that my whole body is shaking.

54
00:04:37,300 --> 00:04:38,640
Oh, never mind.

55
00:04:46,880 --> 00:04:49,200
We're quite a drill team, Gekomon!

56
00:04:50,840 --> 00:04:52,600
Hey, looking good, guys.

57
00:04:58,560 --> 00:05:00,377
Well, that was a good day's work.

58
00:05:00,460 --> 00:05:01,857
Yeah, good work, everyone.

59
00:05:01,940 --> 00:05:04,457
We painted everything in sight
and some things that weren't.

60
00:05:04,540 --> 00:05:07,537
And Davis almost strained his
voice from shouting orders.

61
00:05:07,620 --> 00:05:10,097
You know, even with fresh
paint and some new roofs,

62
00:05:10,180 --> 00:05:12,533
Primary Village still seems kind of sad.

63
00:05:14,040 --> 00:05:14,870
You're right.

64
00:05:15,320 --> 00:05:17,317
Maybe a cute carpenter will come by.

65
00:05:17,400 --> 00:05:19,837
If one shows up, the only
place he's going is to work.

66
00:05:19,920 --> 00:05:21,677
Hi, volunteer to be his helper.

67
00:05:21,760 --> 00:05:25,417
Boy, that jerk really left the digital
world in a big mess, didn't he?

68
00:05:25,500 --> 00:05:27,157
Hey, listen, it's not my fault.

69
00:05:27,240 --> 00:05:28,457
I'm doing the best I can.

70
00:05:28,540 --> 00:05:31,100
I was talking about Ken, not you, Davis.

71
00:05:32,260 --> 00:05:33,420
Oh, never mind.

72
00:05:33,800 --> 00:05:34,800
No harm, no foul.

73
00:05:36,940 --> 00:05:40,080
You gotta wonder, what
is he up to now? Huh.

74
00:05:40,560 --> 00:05:42,917
Is he just playing with
us so we think he's nice?

75
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:46,777
Well, if he really is nice, he'd be here
just like us, cleaning this place up.

76
00:05:46,860 --> 00:05:47,690
That's for sure.

77
00:05:47,773 --> 00:05:51,780
But Ken's not coming back to the
digital world anymore, uh, right?

78
00:05:52,020 --> 00:05:53,314
One wouldn't think so.

79
00:05:53,480 --> 00:05:57,137
Yeah, I don't think the Digimon
could forgive him for what he did.

80
00:05:57,220 --> 00:05:58,280
That's for sure.

81
00:05:59,120 --> 00:06:00,100
You sound mad.

82
00:06:00,620 --> 00:06:01,697
Well, you're right.

83
00:06:01,780 --> 00:06:02,957
I can't forgive him either.

84
00:06:03,040 --> 00:06:03,870
Not ever.

85
00:06:04,040 --> 00:06:05,677
Well, let's get back to the real world.

86
00:06:05,760 --> 00:06:07,380
I'm starving. Great idea!

87
00:06:07,540 --> 00:06:08,720
Davis party of one.

88
00:06:08,900 --> 00:06:09,841
Let's get going.

89
00:06:10,260 --> 00:06:12,337
Well, I got a kendo lesson to get to.

90
00:06:12,420 --> 00:06:13,250
See you later.

91
00:06:13,720 --> 00:06:14,550
Yeah, see ya.

92
00:06:14,820 --> 00:06:17,157
Guess we'll have to
just pig out without ya.

93
00:06:17,240 --> 00:06:19,580
Have fun at your lesson. Thanks.

94
00:06:20,380 --> 00:06:21,800
And now what time is it?

95
00:06:21,960 --> 00:06:23,220
It is time to munch.

96
00:06:24,880 --> 00:06:26,500
Hey, what is this?

97
00:06:27,080 --> 00:06:27,910
Huh?

98
00:06:28,260 --> 00:06:29,090
Hmm.

99
00:06:52,780 --> 00:06:53,820
Excellent, Cody.

100
00:06:54,120 --> 00:06:55,414
This is all for today.

101
00:07:00,280 --> 00:07:01,110
Yes, sir.

102
00:07:01,500 --> 00:07:02,559
Thank you, Sensei.

103
00:07:07,340 --> 00:07:08,170
Grandpa?

104
00:07:10,260 --> 00:07:11,090
Look!

105
00:07:11,173 --> 00:07:12,200
Check these out.

106
00:07:12,380 --> 00:07:14,560
They're liquid yogurt packs. Oh.

107
00:07:15,920 --> 00:07:18,360
I always eat yogurt with a spoon, right?

108
00:07:18,980 --> 00:07:19,810
Wrong.

109
00:07:20,980 --> 00:07:23,497
Last week I said, what the
heck, and tried it this way.

110
00:07:23,580 --> 00:07:24,410
What a surprise.

111
00:07:24,493 --> 00:07:29,161
I learned that I really prefer drinking my
yogurt instead of eating it with a spoon.

112
00:07:30,040 --> 00:07:34,628
A light went on, and I realized we never
stop learning if we just keep trying.

113
00:07:35,820 --> 00:07:37,408
It's an interesting lesson.

114
00:07:52,620 --> 00:07:54,340
Just a little lower, Pormon.

115
00:07:56,300 --> 00:07:57,740
Yeah, that's great.

116
00:08:01,020 --> 00:08:02,160
Finally, I did it.

117
00:08:02,360 --> 00:08:03,417
Wait till Davis sees this.

118
00:08:03,500 --> 00:08:04,330
He'll flip.

119
00:08:11,540 --> 00:08:15,540
And Cat Punzel would let down her
Persian hair so he could climb up.

120
00:08:23,660 --> 00:08:24,497
Grandpa's right.

121
00:08:24,580 --> 00:08:26,227
Being flexible is important.

122
00:08:26,960 --> 00:08:28,520
Monomir shoots! He's gone!

123
00:08:29,320 --> 00:08:30,340
Oh, no.

124
00:08:31,420 --> 00:08:35,437
And if this happens again, young man,
you will be talking to the principal.

125
00:08:35,520 --> 00:08:36,350
Yes, sir.

126
00:08:36,433 --> 00:08:37,374
It won't happen again.

127
00:08:39,240 --> 00:08:41,640
What took you so long? Lose your way?

128
00:08:41,840 --> 00:08:42,899
It's a long story.

129
00:08:43,240 --> 00:08:46,057
Some teachers are just nitpickers
about such little things.

130
00:08:46,140 --> 00:08:47,337
What was it this time?

131
00:08:47,420 --> 00:08:50,877
I was accidentally playing soccer
indoors, and somehow a window broke.

132
00:08:50,960 --> 00:08:51,790
Boy, Mr.

133
00:08:51,873 --> 00:08:53,249
Reynolds sure is a grouch.

134
00:08:53,420 --> 00:08:54,380
Not again.

135
00:08:54,720 --> 00:08:55,937
It's the fourth time.

136
00:08:56,020 --> 00:08:57,257
But hey, who's counting, right?

137
00:08:57,340 --> 00:08:58,170
Really?

138
00:08:58,520 --> 00:09:00,285
I thought it was at least six.

139
00:09:03,940 --> 00:09:05,000
All right, ready?

140
00:09:06,080 --> 00:09:07,920
Back to Primary Village we go!

141
00:09:08,280 --> 00:09:09,600
Fitching board, open!

142
00:09:25,080 --> 00:09:27,220
We have new friends!

143
00:09:27,560 --> 00:09:28,400
Let's play!

144
00:09:28,680 --> 00:09:29,620
Let's play!

145
00:09:29,920 --> 00:09:32,214
Well, they sure weren't here yesterday.

146
00:09:32,380 --> 00:09:34,937
Maybe it's because yesterday
we rebuilt the nursery school.

147
00:09:35,020 --> 00:09:35,850
No way!

148
00:09:35,960 --> 00:09:37,860
That was a nursery school? Yeah.

149
00:09:38,260 --> 00:09:40,577
So logic dictates they
were all students there.

150
00:09:40,660 --> 00:09:41,490
I gotcha.

151
00:09:41,573 --> 00:09:43,397
So if we restore things
to the way they were,

152
00:09:43,480 --> 00:09:45,517
then all of the Digimon
will return as well,

153
00:09:45,600 --> 00:09:46,430
right?

154
00:09:47,340 --> 00:09:49,046
Right, so let's keep working.

155
00:09:49,300 --> 00:09:50,580
They're a major pain.

156
00:09:53,180 --> 00:09:54,600
Aw, they're cute.

157
00:09:56,540 --> 00:09:58,160
Oh, they're so sweet.

158
00:09:58,820 --> 00:10:00,996
Guess not what you said a second ago.

159
00:10:02,340 --> 00:10:04,869
Vimon, let's give this
our best shot today.

160
00:10:05,020 --> 00:10:06,196
You know what to do!

161
00:10:31,060 --> 00:10:31,940
Uh-oh!

162
00:10:32,160 --> 00:10:33,437
And what was that all about?

163
00:10:33,520 --> 00:10:34,755
You gotta try harder!

164
00:10:35,020 --> 00:10:36,960
Digiball! Hmph! Digimon!

165
00:10:38,760 --> 00:10:40,420
Digimon! Digimon!

166
00:10:40,640 --> 00:10:41,640
Digital Monsters!

167
00:10:41,760 --> 00:10:43,289
Digimon are the champions!

168
00:10:43,860 --> 00:10:44,690
Digimon!

169
00:10:44,773 --> 00:10:45,780
Digital Monsters!

170
00:10:45,900 --> 00:10:47,429
Digimon are the champions!

171
00:10:50,700 --> 00:10:52,260
Vimon! Digiball!

172
00:10:56,660 --> 00:10:57,490
Vimon!

173
00:10:57,660 --> 00:10:59,120
I'm still Vimon!

174
00:11:01,860 --> 00:11:04,337
I guess my biorhythm's off today!

175
00:11:04,420 --> 00:11:05,597
This doesn't make sense.

176
00:11:05,680 --> 00:11:08,504
You ate a ton and you
Digivolve fine yesterday.

177
00:11:09,500 --> 00:11:11,200
Why can't I digivol?

178
00:11:11,540 --> 00:11:13,020
Tell me why!

179
00:11:16,320 --> 00:11:17,150
I'm done.

180
00:11:17,233 --> 00:11:18,497
Maybe you're just tired.

181
00:11:18,580 --> 00:11:20,537
Maybe it's some kind of digi jet lag.

182
00:11:20,620 --> 00:11:23,197
Is there any chance you
ate some bad chocolate?

183
00:11:23,280 --> 00:11:24,110
Nope.

184
00:11:24,480 --> 00:11:26,297
Maybe chicken soup might help, huh?

185
00:11:26,380 --> 00:11:27,397
But his stomach's full.

186
00:11:27,480 --> 00:11:29,137
He had four TV dinners for lunch.

187
00:11:29,220 --> 00:11:30,220
Maybe he overate.

188
00:11:30,440 --> 00:11:31,357
I got it.

189
00:11:31,440 --> 00:11:33,297
He hasn't fought in so
long that he's rusty.

190
00:11:33,380 --> 00:11:36,477
It's like he's totally out of
shape when it comes to digivolving.

191
00:11:36,560 --> 00:11:37,397
That must be it.

192
00:11:37,480 --> 00:11:38,715
Glad I thought of it.

193
00:11:39,920 --> 00:11:43,497
You just need to relax and do something
to take your mind off of this.

194
00:11:43,580 --> 00:11:46,877
We have decided that both of you
need a break from all this hard work.

195
00:11:46,960 --> 00:11:47,790
So guess what?

196
00:11:47,873 --> 00:11:49,857
They're going back to work
while the four of us rest.

197
00:11:49,940 --> 00:11:50,770
Great.

198
00:11:50,853 --> 00:11:54,534
Well, I didn't want to say anything,
but I could use a little nap myself.

199
00:11:54,960 --> 00:11:56,317
We're going to go outside of town.

200
00:11:56,400 --> 00:11:57,917
We have to check something out.

201
00:11:58,000 --> 00:11:59,657
We'll be back in about an hour.

202
00:11:59,740 --> 00:12:00,697
Hey, I could use a little nap.

203
00:12:00,780 --> 00:12:02,297
Hey, I got a great idea.

204
00:12:02,380 --> 00:12:05,117
Yeah, I know something we could
do that's really relaxing.

205
00:12:05,200 --> 00:12:06,030
Ha!

206
00:12:06,113 --> 00:12:06,943
Yo, Lee!

207
00:12:07,280 --> 00:12:08,480
No go for Gato.

208
00:12:08,900 --> 00:12:10,537
All right, Panamon, you try it.

209
00:12:10,620 --> 00:12:12,208
Go ahead, give it your all.

210
00:12:12,300 --> 00:12:13,130
Great.

211
00:12:28,100 --> 00:12:29,453
Panamon, Digivolve, go!

212
00:12:35,940 --> 00:12:37,120
I need a break.

213
00:12:37,440 --> 00:12:38,637
We're not amateurs like V-Man.

214
00:12:38,720 --> 00:12:39,617
What's going on?

215
00:12:39,700 --> 00:12:40,677
It's pretty strange.

216
00:12:40,760 --> 00:12:41,590
Boy.

217
00:12:44,420 --> 00:12:48,097
I thought the control spires had been
knocked out of commission when Ken left.

218
00:12:48,180 --> 00:12:51,474
But this makes me think they
just might be active again.

219
00:13:28,840 --> 00:13:32,437
DIGMOND and I are going to get back to
work and finish fixing those water lines.

220
00:13:32,520 --> 00:13:33,350
Okay.

221
00:13:33,500 --> 00:13:36,457
Hey, how is it Veeamon and
I end up having to babysit?

222
00:13:36,540 --> 00:13:37,370
Well,

223
00:13:37,453 --> 00:13:39,357
someone has to look after
them, and strange as it sounds,

224
00:13:39,440 --> 00:13:43,381
they really seem to like you, so
we figured you're the best choice.

225
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:44,830
See?

226
00:13:46,080 --> 00:13:47,740
Wait! Hey! See ya!

227
00:13:56,460 --> 00:13:57,290
Oh, boy.

228
00:13:57,660 --> 00:13:58,660
Now what do I do?

229
00:14:01,940 --> 00:14:02,940
All right, watch.

230
00:14:03,140 --> 00:14:04,657
You control the ball like this.

231
00:14:04,740 --> 00:14:05,880
Got it? It's easy.

232
00:14:06,260 --> 00:14:07,495
Here it comes, V-Mon.

233
00:14:09,020 --> 00:14:09,850
Got it!

234
00:14:11,060 --> 00:14:13,589
Okay, now let's play
a game so you can try.

235
00:14:14,240 --> 00:14:15,600
Come on, get the ball!

236
00:14:17,260 --> 00:14:18,789
Pass the damage, I'm open!

237
00:14:20,780 --> 00:14:22,500
Go after that ball!

238
00:14:25,220 --> 00:14:26,917
Nice, you're getting the hang of it.

239
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:27,830
Great!

240
00:14:31,320 --> 00:14:35,040
Go! Go! Go! That's it! Use
your head! Outstanding!

241
00:14:35,500 --> 00:14:36,500
We're back, Yoli.

242
00:14:36,900 --> 00:14:38,665
Looks like they're having fun.

243
00:14:38,940 --> 00:14:43,587
I told Davis to watch the little ones,
though I'm not sure who's watching whom.

244
00:14:45,780 --> 00:14:47,300
What a great idea.

245
00:14:47,560 --> 00:14:51,177
This way, Davis gets a chance to boss
around someone else besides just us.

246
00:14:51,260 --> 00:14:52,520
So, give me the 411.

247
00:14:52,920 --> 00:14:54,508
Were you able to digivolve?

248
00:14:55,080 --> 00:14:55,910
Nope.

249
00:14:55,993 --> 00:14:59,700
I think the control spires are
what's keeping them from Digivolving.

250
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:02,700
So, we gotta find a
way to prove my theory.

251
00:15:03,300 --> 00:15:04,560
Where's Cody, anyway?

252
00:15:06,460 --> 00:15:08,940
Boy, the Emperor sure left a mess here.

253
00:15:09,540 --> 00:15:11,011
Everything's been buried.

254
00:15:11,120 --> 00:15:12,591
Well, we got a lot to do.

255
00:15:12,680 --> 00:15:14,798
I guess we better get started, Cody.

256
00:15:16,320 --> 00:15:17,150
Cody?

257
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:18,830
Oh.

258
00:15:18,913 --> 00:15:21,880
Uh, yeah, let's get to
work, Digmon. Dig it!

259
00:15:29,720 --> 00:15:30,680
It's Thundermon!

260
00:15:30,900 --> 00:15:33,920
He's a highly charged
Digimon who's electrifying.

261
00:15:34,140 --> 00:15:35,797
But what's shocking about this pint-sized

262
00:15:35,880 --> 00:15:39,180
powerhouse is that he packs
a 10 million volt wallop.

263
00:15:44,920 --> 00:15:45,937
I don't understand.

264
00:15:46,020 --> 00:15:47,337
Why is he attacking us?

265
00:15:47,420 --> 00:15:50,597
He's not being directed by a control
spire or wearing a dark ring.

266
00:15:50,680 --> 00:15:51,737
I just don't get it.

267
00:15:51,820 --> 00:15:52,650
What should we do?

268
00:15:52,733 --> 00:15:54,377
I'm not sure if we should fight.

269
00:15:54,460 --> 00:15:55,290
Ahh!

270
00:15:59,560 --> 00:16:00,390
